FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/testing/harness.rs:208-221 — a *successful* `// bench` records nothing in `TestResults`: `run_benchmark` prints `BENCHMARK`/`ns/iter` and returns `Ok(())` without calling `add_pass`/`add_skip`, so `results.total == 0`. `src/lib.rs:159-175` then makes `reject_zero_test_run` reject it with `No tests were defined in '<file>'`, and `rb test <file>` (or a project whose only `.rb` holds a passing benchmark) exits 1. This is an error regression: before the zero-execution guard a passing benchmark file exited 0, and round 6 only fixed the *failing* benchmark case (`edge_rb_test_reports_a_failing_benchmark`), leaving the symmetric success case broken and mislabelled. Fix: record a pass (or a distinct executed-benchmark count) for a successful benchmark so the guard cannot mistake it for an empty file, and add `edge_rb_test_reports_a_passing_benchmark` asserting exit 0. VERIFIED: traced the success path at harness.rs:208-221 (only `println!`, no `self.results` mutation) into lib.rs:160 (`if results.passed + results.failed == 0`); no test pins the passing-benchmark behaviour, only the failing one.

2. [MINOR] src/lib.rs:208-215 — `run_test(None)` walks the suite twice: it calls `testing::find_test_files(&tests)?` purely to test emptiness, then `testing::run_tests_in(&tests)?` calls `find_test_files` again (src/testing/mod.rs:68). The first listing is discarded, so every project `rb test` pays two full recursive directory walks, and a file appearing/disappearing between the walks can make the “No Redblue test files” pre-check disagree with what is actually executed. Fix: have `run_tests_in` return the files it ran (or accept a pre-collected list), or drop the pre-check and let the zero-count result carry the “no test files” distinction.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
